00:00:06.812 --> 00:00:09.270
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Let's start
with the Google Cloud migration

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.170
journey.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.930
We have been showing this to
customers for a very long time.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.468
And as more and more customers
migrate their workloads

00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:18.510
to Google Cloud, we've
been able to validate this

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.430
over, and over, and over again.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.560
And the key thing
to this, the reason

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.600
we keep talking about
that is because it works.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.280
It takes an inherently
complex and monstrous process

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.920
of a cloud migration
and puts it down

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.890
into very manageable and
executable chunks of work.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.420
Now, we know that this has
to happen in specific order.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.910
We discover and assess
our application landscape.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.950
We build the foundation
of GCP services

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.950
from IAM, and networking,
and basic architecture.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.350
Then we actually perform
the migration itself.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.670
And once that migration is done,
you assess all of the things

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.758
that you assumed in the process.

00:00:55.758 --> 00:00:57.300
And you go ahead
and start optimizing

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.160
for cost, or performance,
or capability,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.840
or whatever your
application needs.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.220
And then it's also
important to remember

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.345
that there are multiple
ways that we can get there.

00:01:04.345 --> 00:01:06.090
You can do traditional
Lift and Shift,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.250
where you just want to
lower the overall complexity

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.860
and increase the speed of
moving services to the cloud.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.450
You can do the Improve
and Move, where

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.335
you want to refactor
some local code in order

00:01:14.335 --> 00:01:15.960
to take advantage of
cloud native tools

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.375
before you actually
migrate the services up.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:20.250
Or you can do Rip and
Replace, where you just

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.070
want to start over with
the full toolkit of cloud

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.780
at your fingertips.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.492
But the key thing to this--

00:01:26.492 --> 00:01:28.950
and as anyone in the room who's
ever done a cloud migration

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.910
can tell you--

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.240
is we all hear that story
of no two cloud migrations

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.080
are ever the same.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.535
And I'm going to clarify
that statement for the room.

00:01:36.535 --> 00:01:38.410
There's a personal stand
I want to take here,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.720
which is that all successful
cloud migrations are

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.430
unique in their own
way because you all

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.190
have different technologies,
and industry verticals,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.690
and data centers, and
all of these things.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.500
But all failed
cloud migrations all

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.270
fail for the exact same reason.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.340
They either didn't have
a plan to begin with,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.685
or they didn't stick to the
plan that they built on.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:00.060
And we know that
these things are

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.550
incredibly easy to talk
about and incredibly

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.410
hard to actually execute.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.370
And when we see a customer
that comes through, follows

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.990
this journey, makes
a plan, and has

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.990
a passion for learning,
exploring, and testing,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.730
we see success.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.830
And today is the story
of one such customer

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.330
in Kroger Digital.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.580
I'd like to introduce Joe
Timmons, a technical account

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.460
