00:11:47.478 --> 00:11:48.740
I'm really glad for you.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:50.330
Tear it down and do it again.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:51.800
And do it again,
and do it again.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.770
And the team was fantastic.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.690
They did a great job with that.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:12:00.240
So as we did this, we started
to see our mindset change.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.930
We went from this on-prem
pets sort of mentality,

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:10.610
right, to one where we were
expecting transient resources,

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:14.450
and things like that, and being
able to move workloads around

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.237
very easily.

00:12:16.237 --> 00:12:18.570
Some of the other things that
we kind of struggled with,

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.680
more of as a global
Kroger we, is things

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:22.820
like data classification.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:23.870
What's safe?

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:26.150
What are things we feel
comfortable moving out

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:26.760
to the cloud?

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.052
This is our first thing, right?

00:12:28.052 --> 00:12:30.010
What are things that we
feel comfortable moving

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:30.620
to the cloud?

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.693
Search was obviously
a pretty easy decision

00:12:33.693 --> 00:12:36.110
to make because all that stuff's
available on the internet

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:36.800
today, right?

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.425
So there's not a whole lot we're
going to be throwing out there

00:12:39.425 --> 00:12:42.830
that everybody who's crawling
us doesn't already have, right?

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.370
And then also being able to
trust and empower our engineers

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:48.862
to do things that maybe they
haven't done before, right?

00:12:48.862 --> 00:12:50.570
If you think about
on-prem, right, you've

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.090
got a team of guys that
are standing up VMs.

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.460
You have people
installing the OS

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.080
and doing that kind
of thing, right?

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:58.480
Where this is different, right?

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:01.310
We've got Kubernetes, and
we've got these [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:02.990
containers of things.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:08.820
And we are really embracing the
DevOps on both sides of that,

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:09.320
right?

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.120
I mean, development
and operations--

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:16.560
our team has really done a great
job supporting both of those.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.230
So it's not all unicorns
and rainbows, though.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:24.080
We had quite a few speed bumps.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:28.340
Networking-- getting networks
installed into our on-prem data

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:33.830
centers and things like that was
sometimes a bit of a challenge.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:37.518
And in fact, I think
one of our data centers,

00:13:37.518 --> 00:13:39.560
we didn't even have the
right kind of fiber line.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:41.935
And so we had to run a fiber
line or something out there.

00:13:41.935 --> 00:13:44.582
It's all these things that
you don't think about, right,

00:13:44.582 --> 00:13:46.040
when you're building--
when you go,

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.562
oh, yeah, we're going to move
an application out there.

00:13:48.562 --> 00:13:51.020
And then you've got to think,
what does this actually mean?

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.280
We're going to put
customers on this stuff.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.022
What do we have to have, right?

00:13:55.022 --> 00:13:56.480
And these are all
these intangibles

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:58.022
that you've got to
think about as you

00:13:58.022 --> 00:14:00.200
start moving your
applications out there, right?

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.060
It's kind of a big deal.

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.540
Some simple things, like even
our CI/CD pipelines, right?

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.630
On our on-prem solution,
we're deploying our containers

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:10.760
in a blue green fashion.

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.680
And it's something we
don't even think about.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:13.660
It's in-house tooling.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:17.750
OK, we go to GCP, and we're
deploying our applications

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.570
to our Kubernetes clusters.

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:23.030
And OK, wow, how are we going
to do blue green deployment?

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.610
Because this is expected
for what we have.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:28.640
So this is also kind of
things that sort of snuck up

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.095
on us as we moved along.

00:14:33.095 --> 00:14:35.420
And I could just kind of
keep going, and going,

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:36.525
and going on that thing.

00:14:36.525 --> 00:14:38.150
The important thing
here is when you're

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:41.690
doing your discovery phase,
think about the stuff

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:43.418
that you take for
granted when you're

00:14:43.418 --> 00:14:44.960
trying to plan this
stuff out and put

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.240
your timelines together, right?

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:49.910
So given that, I'm going
to pass the mic over here

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:51.440
to Tony DiLoreto.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:55.280
He is a cloud
consultant from Google.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.240
And we worked very closely
with Tony and his team

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:02.650
to make all this stuff happen.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:03.970
TONY DILORETO: Thanks, Jeff.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.100
So yes, as Jeff said, my
name is Tony DiLoreto.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:07.900
I was the cloud
consultant working

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:10.630
with Jeff's team to help
the migration and all

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:11.800
the architecture.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.600
I want to walk you
through now just how we

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:18.310
thought about building out
this new system on GCP, right?

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.920
But before we could even
move the first byte,

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.230
we had to have a plan in
place, like we talked about.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:26.260
And we wanted to make sure that
we would cover all the bases,

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.390
and we had everything in
the cloud ready to go.

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:32.530
So to do so, I wanted to
focus on five key points.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.740
And they all begin with an
S. I call them the five S's.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:36.772
Simplification, right?

00:15:36.772 --> 00:15:38.230
You'll see in a
second what we went

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:40.000
from to what we ended up with.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.840
Security-- making sure it
was secure in the cloud.

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.120
Stability-- being
able to grow and scale

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.325
with our customer base.

00:15:47.325 --> 00:15:48.700
Kroger, being a
national company,

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:51.005
has customers all
over the United States

00:15:51.005 --> 00:15:52.630
and in the world
coming to the website,

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.150
so we wanted to make sure
we could grow with that.

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.400
Data science, the
science bit, and finally,

00:15:57.400 --> 00:15:59.080
microservices, right?

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.930
All of these different pieces--

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:03.550
what we had to incorporate into
the architecture to make sure

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.100
that this application could
grow with Kroger and its

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:06.910
customers' needs.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:14.630
So as we already saw, this
is what I walked into.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.710
And again, probably
not dissimilar to what

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.200
many other people here
are using today, right?

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:21.760
We have sort of this
search Frankenstack,

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:24.140
if you will, with a lot
of different platforms,

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.300
where the teams had to manage
all the different releases

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:30.020
and patches in
security and platforms,

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.550
and making sure that all
of the infrastructure

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.320
was set up for it, and ports.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.500
The list goes on and on.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.250
This is obviously one
of the true benefits

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.200
of migrating to the
cloud, is not having

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.973
to deal with all of that.

00:16:41.973 --> 00:16:43.390
But this is where
we started with.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:46.140
This was our beginning
point of the roadmap.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:48.390
And there were a lot
of different areas

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:50.900
of which we had to ensure
that we incorporated

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.560
into this architecture not only
just the platforms themselves

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:57.353
and the technology, but testing
and validation, rolling out,

00:16:57.353 --> 00:16:58.770
being able to make
sure there were

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:00.373
no single points of failure.

00:17:00.373 --> 00:17:02.790
And all the while, ensuring
that the data was at the right

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.890
place at the right time.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:07.780
So like I said, the
first thing we focused on

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.720
was the simplification
of this architecture.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.130
We wanted to go from this
multi-tenant multi-platform

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.410
stack into something
straightforward.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:19.780
And as you know, probably
with any application,

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:23.230
there are a few key elements or
key phases to the architecture

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:26.280
that it needs to include.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.760
So, this is the
initial sort of look

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.950
at what we had to
migrate to, incorporating

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:36.150
those key tenets, the ingestion
of data coming in from Kroger.

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:38.100
The transformation of the data--

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:40.560
how are we going to
prepare it for being used?

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.740
Storage in some vehicle
within Google Cloud,

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:45.330
and then like Jeff
mentioned, running

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.010
on Kubernetes, or
the application

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:50.640
layer of which all the customers
could then interact with.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.730
Again, this is a very
solid baseline template

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.260
for almost any
application running

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:57.135
in the cloud because it
does incorporate many

00:17:57.135 --> 00:17:59.670
of those key tenets
of a pipeline

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.390
or of an application
running in the cloud.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.710
One of the best things that
Google has the advantage

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:07.948
is to come in and just
drop in and replace some

00:18:07.948 --> 00:18:09.240
of the existing infrastructure.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.510
We are able to identify in that
previous architecture which

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:14.880
of these elements lined up
to the different hexagons

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.850
and the different
verticals you see in here.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.060
Doing so allowed us to
iterate fast, sometimes

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:25.160
fail fast, but iterate rapidly
and to continue to move on.

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:27.193
The next thing we
focused on was stability.

00:18:27.193 --> 00:18:29.360
Again, I wanted to make
sure that we had the ability

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.417
to grow and handle
traffic as Kroger grew.

00:18:31.417 --> 00:18:33.500
As you saw at the very
beginning, Kroger has been.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:35.458
And we wanted to be able
to capture all of that

00:18:35.458 --> 00:18:39.020
and to deliver those low latency
and high durability stability

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:40.890
requirements.

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.820
So we could have
easily have just taken

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:46.010
a visual architecture and
just copied into three zones

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:47.450
and been done with it.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:49.700
But anybody who's been using
any sort of cloud service

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.260
or managed service
in the past knows

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:55.060
that this isn't the
optimal way of doing so.

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:56.670
Where Google really
shines, again,

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:59.300
is having those managed services
that scale with the customers,

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:01.460
where you don't have to
worry about bringing up

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:03.620
your own instances
of them for things

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:07.560
such as Pub/Sub at the very
beginning, or a data flow.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.290
So what we were able to
do is we iterated on this,

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:14.030
but having it in multiple
regions allowed that layer

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:16.550
to be able to grow, and
scale, and handle customers

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:19.780
wherever they were.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:22.833
The next thing we wanted to do
was focus on scaling this out.

00:19:22.833 --> 00:19:25.000
So we took that architecture
that we just looked at,

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.550
and we sort of condensed
it down to what we really

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:28.810
wanted to focus on--

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.325
really, what is
Kroger's IP and what

00:19:30.325 --> 00:19:31.450
they should be bringing in.

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.570
And that's really in that
application layer, right?

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.737
All of the other hexagons
to the left, that's Google

00:19:35.737 --> 00:19:38.680
managed services that we've
built, and tried and true

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.040
and tested.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.700
So let's really focus on
what makes the Kroger search

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:44.530
application unique.

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.210
So what we were
able to do is focus

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.610
on deploying applications
in a rolling manner

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:51.880
into each of the different
regions within Google

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.760
Kubernetes Engine.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:57.370
Once we're able to do that
initially through a test,

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.260
we wanted to focus
more on the service

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:01.300
aspect, that
service-oriented architecture

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.780
that you always hear about.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:06.120
So we're able to break
it up into multiple apps.

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:08.670
And every app could then
do what it was made for.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:12.480
Imagine moving a giant monolith
to a more nimble and service

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:15.030
focused application itself.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.820
This pattern is very simple
to then grow within scale.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:21.600
And as you could see, once
you have one app down,

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.513
you can easily replicate
it to many others.

00:20:24.513 --> 00:20:26.180
This architecture
that you end up seeing

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.422
is not dissimilar from what
we ended up pushing forward.

00:20:29.422 --> 00:20:31.130
And again, it is a
tried and true pattern

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:33.530
that you can then use for
any of your applications.

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:35.660
Kubernetes allowed
us to scale, and it

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:37.610
allowed us to be
able to really focus

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.650
on what Kroger needed to do.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:44.270
The next thing we had to
focus on was security.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:46.353
Obviously, security is
very important to everybody

00:20:46.353 --> 00:20:49.250
that's using Google Cloud.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:50.655
So some of the
key considerations

00:20:50.655 --> 00:20:52.280
that we thought about
when building out

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.850
this first initial
architecture of using Google

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:58.945
managed services are number
one, if you can use Google

00:20:58.945 --> 00:21:01.070
managed services, it takes
that responsibility away

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:03.320
from having a single
point or single person

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.795
worrying about managing
different instances.

00:21:05.795 --> 00:21:07.670
The next thing were
service accounts as well.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.080
Service accounts were
deployed throughout.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.440
And again, it's one
of our best practices.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:14.540
By enabling extensive
logging and monitoring,

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:16.580
we're able to get at
any issues immediately

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:21.120
to be able to detect and to
be able to optimize around.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:22.873
Finally, key
management, making sure

00:21:22.873 --> 00:21:24.540
that all the data
that was going through

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:27.090
was secure at rest
and in transit.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:28.950
And finally, we also
use managed node pools

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.770
to be able to ensure that
we had enough architecture

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:35.192
or enough infrastructure
when we needed it.

00:21:35.192 --> 00:21:36.900
And finally, the last
thing we focused on

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:38.460
was the science aspect, right?

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:41.280
Everybody, again, wants
to get to the AI, the ML.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:44.070
But after we were able to
build out this architecture,

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.030
then we could really
dive into that.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:48.740
So some of the
different areas that

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:52.160
are opportunities for doing data
science with an architecture,

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:56.540
one was real time ingestion
or streaming analytics.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:59.920
So when the data was coming in,
do we see any issues with that?

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:02.170
Second was data
quality analytics

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:04.780
once we're doing our
transformation on the data.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:07.270
Did we notice that any of our
providers or upstream sources

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:10.720
had issues or was
delivering at the wrong time

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:12.870
that we needed to correct?

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:15.810
Third was any sorts of
prescriptive or predictive

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:19.610
analytics on top of the
data once it was in storage.

00:22:19.610 --> 00:22:22.870
Being able to do modeling
and be able to get at really

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.370
what are customers' data is key.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:28.540
And then finally, the usage of
customer analytics on the app

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:29.373
itself--

00:22:29.373 --> 00:22:31.040
where are they
interacting with the app?

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:33.460
What are their points
of confusion or areas

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:36.453
that we can then improve
for the next iteration?

00:22:36.453 --> 00:22:38.120
And what that, I'm
going to turn it back

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.412
over to Jeff to talk a little
about the lessons learned

00:22:40.412 --> 00:22:41.330
and the next steps.

00:22:46.762 --> 00:22:48.470
JEFFREY HECHT: Yeah,
so lessons learned--

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:50.970
I don't think I have enough
time to go through all

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.570
the lessons learned, right?

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:57.020
But some of the important
takeaways, right?

00:22:57.020 --> 00:23:00.560
It's important to understand
that my focus and my team's

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:03.830
focus is primarily on search,
and relevance, and things

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:04.440
like that.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:05.990
And one of the important
things that we're doing

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:07.680
is we're doing actual
data science now

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:12.260
to let our customers'
actions on our site talk

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:14.990
to us about what
types of products

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:18.740
we should be returning based
on those types of search terms.

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:23.810
And that's that AI,
ML thing that Tony

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.490
was talking about there.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.410
And that's real big
for us with what

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:29.838
we're doing in
some Dataproc stuff

00:23:29.838 --> 00:23:31.130
that we have set up internally.

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:32.600
We have a system called Agatha.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:35.910
And so we're actually doing
real data science on this

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:39.338
to be able to do
this type of stuff.

00:23:39.338 --> 00:23:40.880
The other thing
that's great is we're

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:43.070
able to launch
completely new stacks,

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:46.670
fully populate them with
content and everything,

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:48.830
literally with the
click of a button.

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:54.590
One of the guys on my team,
Jim, literally, on Saturday,

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:58.670
spun up a new environment that
we needed for some testing.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:02.010
And the whole thing took an
hour to build the entire search

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:03.020
stack.

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:06.320
So that goes back to that whole
infrastructure as code piece.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:07.555
It's huge, right?

00:24:07.555 --> 00:24:08.930
The other nice
thing that you can

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:11.510
probably saw on those diagrams
that Tony had up there,

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:14.160
but is the scaling
factor of this.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:17.900
We literally can scale
this thing out to infinity.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:22.070
It's really easy for
us to do that now.

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:24.560
And then the new search
platform-- and a lot of this

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:26.750
is based on some of the
GCP tools that we're using.

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:28.640
A lot of this is also based
on the re-architecture

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:30.015
that we did of
the search engine.

00:24:30.015 --> 00:24:32.570
But we can handle
orders of magnitude

00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:36.740
more volume than we
did before with just

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:40.130
an amazing improvement in
response time and stuff.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:44.010
So it's the whole
re-architecture.

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:50.100
And then dropping it on GCP was
just of absolute benefit to us.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:51.860
So, right.

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:53.570
What does this mean,
then, for the future

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:54.800
of Kroger on the cloud?

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:58.370
There's a ton of work
to do here, right?

00:24:58.370 --> 00:25:01.850
Lots of workloads to go,
more than I can talk about.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.320
Here are some of
those potentially

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:06.473
Lift and Shift, some of those
re-architecture type things.

00:25:06.473 --> 00:25:08.390
But the important thing
to take away from this

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:12.920
is that our application really
broke down a lot of barriers

00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:15.170
for the teams that are sort
of riding on our coattails

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:16.620
and following along.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:20.870
So that helps to accelerate
the rest of those applications

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.840
and those teams, which is
super important for Kroger

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:28.550
and ultimately our customers
to be able to take that

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:30.710
to the next level.

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:33.320
And then the other
nice thing that's

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:38.420
about it, working with
the Google guys, is--

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:40.790
if I could put this in one
sentence, I think it was,

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:43.580
how can I help you
help Kroger, right?

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:46.040
I can't tell you how many
times Tony would give me a call

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:49.813
and say, hey, I've got these
guys from BQ, from BigQuery.

00:25:49.813 --> 00:25:51.230
And they want to
understand what's

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:53.517
important to you for how
you're doing your Machine

00:25:53.517 --> 00:25:55.350
Learning, and your AI,
and things like that.

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:58.293
And we'd hop on a phone call
and have these discussions.

00:25:58.293 --> 00:25:59.960
And that's the first
time that I've ever

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:04.550
had a partner get
with me and say,

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.380
I want to make your job easier.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:07.650
What can I do to do this?

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.160
It's usually me
saying, hey, this thing

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:13.370
that we're trying
to use doesn't work.

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:16.730
Or I need to pound this square
peg through this round hole.

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:20.490
And so that's been fantastic.

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.850
I've really enjoyed that, too.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:24.740
And it's kind of
cool to also think

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:28.730
that things that are
important to us as Kroger

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:32.690
might actually be influencing
products and things that Google

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:36.410
is going to develop someday that
might be beneficial to you guys

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:36.910
as well.

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:39.590
So that's pretty great.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:40.580
That's pretty cool.

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:44.530
